<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cree</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cree people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cree are one of the largest Indigenous peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, speaking Algonquian languages, with lands stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> lowlands to the northern plains. They were key partners of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>, and the Plains Cree joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assiniboine">Assiniboine</a> in the Iron Confederacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cree.jpg" alt="Cree" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noahedits · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cree_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word Cree comes into English from the French Cris, short for Christinaux, which came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> word Kinistino. The Ojibwe used it for the many communities they met north and west of Lake Superior, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a>. The Cree are generally divided into groups by dialect and region, among them the Plains Cree, Woodland Cree, Rocky Cree, Swampy Cree, Moose Cree and East Cree, and the Atikamekw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innu">Innu</a> and Naskapi are closely related.</p><p>Cree homelands cover much of eastern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, from Eeyou Istchee in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, much of the Canadian Prairies, and into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> and the Northwest Territories. The Cree number more than 350,000 in Canada, where they are one of the largest First Nations groupings. Small communities live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> the Cree were first contacted by Europeans in 1682, at the mouth of the Nelson and Hayes rivers, by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> party travelling about 160 kilometres inland. In 1732 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-gaultier-de-varennes-sieur-de-la-verendrye">Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye</a>, met 200 Cree warriors in what is now north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, near present-day Fort Frances, in war paint for an attack on the Dakota and another group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a>.</p><p>A westward movement over time is strongly associated with the Cree role as traders and hunters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a>. After acquiring firearms from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> they moved onto the plains as traders and acted as middlemen with the company. The Cree and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwa</a> were displaced from a large area north of Winnipeg by the Canadian government between 1905 and the 1930s. The Numbered Treaties cover most Cree homelands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Historically the Cree were hunter-gatherers. The basic units were the lodge, perhaps eight to a dozen people, usually the families of two related married couples sharing a wigwam or tipi, and the band, a group of lodges that moved and hunted together. Lodges could leave bands, and bands formed and dissolved with ease. Beyond regional gatherings there was no higher formal structure, and decisions of war and peace were made by consensus. Clans each had a representative and a vote in important councils. Cree is a group of closely related Algonquian languages, a chain of dialects in which neighbours understand each other easily, and the most widely spoken Indigenous language in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cree” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Inuit traded with peoples to the south, but boundary disputes were frequent, and the Chukchi, Siberian Yupik, Gwichʼin, <b>Cree</b> and Innu kept them from moving far south of the tree line.</blockquote><a href="/culture/inuit">Inuit · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late 16th and early 17th centuries Dakota and Lakota speakers lived in the upper Mississippi region, in what is now Minnesota and Wisconsin, until <b>Cree</b> war parties armed with iron weapons pushed them west onto the Great Plains.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The words have been translated into Inuktitut, Ojibwe, <b>Cree</b> and Mi'kmaq, and there is a trilingual version in English, French and Inuktitut; Daniel Tlen sang it in Southern Tutchone at the opening of the 1988 Winter Olympics in Calgary, and in February 2007 Akina Shirt became the first…</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-canada">O Canada</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Cree</b> uprising of the North-West Rebellion, Cree men led by Wandering Spirit attacked the small settlement of Frog Lake, in the District of Saskatchewan of the North-West Territories, and killed nine officials, clergy and settlers.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-2">2 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 18th century they acquired horses from the south and guns from <b>Cree</b> middlemen trading with the Hudson's Bay Company, and the Siksika, Kainai and Piikani emerged as an allied military power.</blockquote><a href="/state/blackfoot-confederacy">Blackfoot Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Cree</b> and the Assiniboine, or Nakoda, had long been allies, and in the 18th century they became the main middlemen between the Hudson's Bay Company posts on Hudson Bay and peoples of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
